{
  "summary": "The previous rounds converge on a reasonable extraction taxonomy but suffer from a collective blind spot: none of them address the ordering problem created by Textual's reactive attribute system and the @work decorator. Specifically, 15+ methods decorated with @work (e.g., _run_plan_async, _run_implement_async, _run_script_async, _auto_plan_feature) are coroutines that Textual schedules on the app's worker thread pool — they can only be defined on App subclasses or widgets, not on extracted free-standing async functions or a separate PipelineController class, because @work relies on self.call_from_thread() and self.workers for lifecycle management. Round 3's suggestion to 'just move them into free async functions accepting PipelineApp' silently breaks Textual's worker cancellation semantics: when PipelineApp calls self.workers.cancel_group(), it only finds workers registered on itself, not workers spawned by imported functions. This means the proposed Tier 2 extraction of action functions is not as mechanical as claimed — every @work-decorated method needs either a wrapper that delegates back to PipelineApp.run_worker(), or the extracted module must use Textual's message posting (self.post_message) pattern, which Round 3 mentioned but didn't reconcile with the 'zero new abstractions' promise. Additionally, the _check_for_changes bug identified in Round 3 (FeatureFile.list_all() re-stating all boards on every cycle) is real and should be fixed independently before any refactor, but the proposed fix of 'caching all-features and refreshing on board-switch' introduces a stale-cache invalidation problem for server push — remote clients would miss features created on non-active boards until the next manual board switch. A simpler fix is to gate the server push on a separate, slower timer (e.g., every 30s) rather than coupling it to per-second change detection. The characterization test debate is also under-specified: Rounds 1-3 all agree tests should come first but none acknowledge that the auto-plan/auto-implement state machine (spanning _auto_run_check, _auto_plan_check, _auto_implement_check, _handle_rate_limit, and 6+ tracking dictionaries like _auto_plan_fail_cooldown, _auto_plan_queued, _auto_plan_fail_count) is the single most complex and least testable subsystem, and is precisely where a refactor would deliver the most value — yet it's buried inside the 'async-worker-launcher' category without special treatment."
}
